      "area": null,
      "group": { "acronym": "none", "name": "Individual Submissions" },
      "keywords": [],
      "pages": 4,
      "abstract": "This RFC gives a description of what the Hostnames Server is and how to access it.  The function of this particular server is to deliver machine-readable name/address information describing networks, gateways, hosts, and eventually domains, within the internet environment."
    },
    "rfc8117": {
      "title": "Current Hostname Practice Considered Harmful",
      "subseries": [],
      "status": "inf",
      "status_name": "informational",
      "stream": "IETF",
      "stream_name": "IETF",
      "obsoletes": [],
      "obsoleted_by": [],
      "updates": [],
      "updated_by": [],
      "authors": ["C. Huitema", "D. Thaler", "R. Winter"],
      "published": "2017-03-10",
      "identifiers": [{ "type": "doi", "value": "10.17487/RFC8117" }],
      "area": { "acronym": "int", "name": "Internet Area" },
      "group": { "acronym": "intarea", "name": "Internet Area Working Group" },
      "keywords": [],
      "pages": 12,
      "abstract": "Giving a hostname to your computer and publishing it as you roam from one network to another is the Internet's equivalent of walking around with a name tag affixed to your lapel. This current practice can significantly compromise your privacy, and something should change in order to mitigate these privacy threats.\n\n There are several possible remedies, such as fixing a variety of protocols or avoiding disclosing a hostname at all. This document describes some of the protocols that reveal hostnames today and sketches another possible remedy, which is to replace static hostnames by frequently changing randomized values."
    },
    "rfc849": {
      "title": "Suggestions for improved host table distribution",
      "subseries": [],
      "status": "unkn",
      "status_name": "unknown",
      "stream": "Legacy",
      "stream_name": "Legacy",
      "obsoletes": [],
      "obsoleted_by": [],
